{"name":"Brazil Nut","hex":"#856765","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brazil nut","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Bertholletia excelsa, a tree native to tropical South America.","The nut produced by this tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brazil_nut"}],"entries":[{"title":"Brazil nut","blocks":[{"text":"Brazil nut refers to a South American tree of the monotypic genus Bertholletia in the family Lecythidaceae as well as the tree's commercially-harvested edible seeds. It is one of the largest and longest-lived trees in the Amazon rainforest. The fruit and its nutshell – containing the edible nut – are relatively large and weigh as much as 2 kg (4.4 lb) in total. As food, Brazil nuts are notable for diverse content of micronutrients, especially a high amount of selenium. The wood of the Brazil nut tree is prized for its quality in carpentry, flooring, and heavy construction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Bertholletia_excelsa_compose.jpg/330px-Bertholletia_excelsa_compose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil_nut","wikidata":"Q81671"},{"title":"Nut (food)","blocks":[{"text":"A nut is a fruit consisting of a hard or tough nutshell protecting a kernel that usually is edible. In general usage and in a culinary sense, many dry seeds are called nuts. In a botanical context, \"nut\" implies that the shell does not open to release the seed (indehiscent)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Chestnut.jpg/330px-Chestnut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nut_(food)","wikidata":"Q3320037"},{"title":"Brazil","blocks":[{"text":"Brazil, officially the Federative Republic of Brazil, is the largest country in South America. It is also the world's fifth-largest country by area and the seventh-largest by population, with over 214 million people. Brazil is a federation composed of 26 states and a Federal District, which hosts the capital, Brasília. Its most populous city is São Paulo, followed by Rio de Janeiro. Brazil has the largest Lusophone population in the world and is the only Portuguese-speaking country in the Americas, where it is the official language."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/05/Flag_of_Brazil.svg/330px-Flag_of_Brazil.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil","wikidata":"Q155"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/COCO_DE_CASTA%C3%91A_%28BERTHOLLETIA_EXCELSA%29.jpg/500px-COCO_DE_CASTA%C3%91A_%28BERTHOLLETIA_EXCELSA%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/COCO_DE_CASTA%C3%91A_%28BERTHOLLETIA_EXCELSA%29.jpg/1280px-COCO_DE_CASTA%C3%91A_%28BERTHOLLETIA_EXCELSA%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/COCO_DE_CASTA%C3%91A_%28BERTHOLLETIA_EXCELSA%29.jpg/500px-COCO_DE_CASTA%C3%91A_%28BERTHOLLETIA_EXCELSA%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Brazil nut fruit attached to branch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACOCO_DE_CASTA%C3%91A_(BERTHOLLETIA_EXCELSA).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Brazil_nut_DSC05477.JPG/500px-Brazil_nut_DSC05477.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Brazil_nut_DSC05477.JPG/1280px-Brazil_nut_DSC05477.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Brazil_nut_DSC05477.JPG/500px-Brazil_nut_DSC05477.JPG?utm_s